/**
 * City coordinate table.
 *
 * Keys are normalised place names (see normalisePlaceKey). `lat`/`lon` are
 * decimal degrees, WGS84, city centre — good to roughly a kilometre, which is
 * far finer than a dot on a national map needs.
 *
 * `region` is shown in the ranked list so a reader can tell two same-named
 * places apart. `note` marks an entry the reader should not take at face
 * value — an approximation or an ambiguous name — and the component prints it
 * beside the row.
 *
 * `plotAt` names the city a place is DRAWN at, while it keeps its own row in
 * the ranked list. It exists because of one hard fact of scale: at national
 * zoom a proportional circle for Delhi is a good 100 km across, so every Delhi
 * colony — Pitampura, Punjabi Bagh, Vasant Kunj, thirty-odd of them — lands
 * inside it. Drawn as separate marks they paint over the middle of the biggest
 * market in the country and it renders as a pale speckled ring: the exact
 * opposite of the truth. So colonies and numbered sectors roll up to their
 * parent city for the map only. Nothing is merged away — every locality keeps
 * its own line in the table, labelled with where it is drawn.
 *
 * Gurgaon, Noida, Faridabad and Ghaziabad do NOT roll up. They are separate
 * markets with their own sales stories, and they get their own marks.
 *
 * Coverage: every place carrying more than two records in the live sales
 * funnel, plus the recognisable cities from the long tail.
 */
